1
00:00:00,053 --> 00:00:04,337
Every day, 11,200 Americans turn 65.

2
00:00:04,337 --> 00:00:08,134
When an expert retires, a screen recording keeps her clicks,

3
00:00:08,134 --> 00:00:10,119
not the judgment behind them.

4
00:00:10,119 --> 00:00:13,567
Vashistha learns that judgment, and proves it transfers.

5
00:00:14,376 --> 00:00:18,881
This is a verified replay of a recorded live run on production;

6
00:00:18,881 --> 00:00:22,433
its hash chain is re-verified on load.

7
00:00:22,433 --> 00:00:25,382
The expert is a synthetic test voice.

8
00:00:25,382 --> 00:00:29,039
A deterministic gate waits until she is silent,

9
00:00:29,039 --> 00:00:32,092
her screen is still and she isn't typing,

10
00:00:32,092 --> 00:00:35,877
then the agent asks the question with the most expected information.

11
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:43,685
[Agent, recorded] What told you to approve onboarding here, and what would have changed your mind?

12
00:00:44,085 --> 00:00:51,573
[Expert, recorded · synthetic voice] Never approve a customer from a high-risk country at desk level unless they've banked with us for two years with verified funds.

13
00:00:53,496 --> 00:00:59,661
That sentence became 2 stop-rules, stored with her exact words and the screen frame.

14
00:00:59,661 --> 00:01:04,200
After each answer the agent stays silent: no valid nonce,

15
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:07,428
so our custom LLM returns skip turn.

16
00:01:08,045 --> 00:01:12,805
Then Z3 searches her rules for valid cases they leave undecided.

17
00:01:12,805 --> 00:01:16,126
In this recorded run, the debrief was left open.

18
00:01:16,631 --> 00:01:20,451
Here it is completed on this build, with typed answers.

19
00:01:20,451 --> 00:01:24,410
Each witness is a concrete case to decide or escalate.

20
00:01:24,410 --> 00:01:27,463
The teach-back uses confirmed rules only.

21
00:01:27,463 --> 00:01:30,667
She corrects a threshold, the solver reruns,

22
00:01:30,667 --> 00:01:33,814
and coverage closes under the current feature model.

23
00:01:36,262 --> 00:01:38,956
The Work Map is built by code.

24
00:01:38,956 --> 00:01:43,646
Every rule traces back through the ledger to a frame, a decision,

25
00:01:43,646 --> 00:01:45,689
and the expert's own words.

26
00:01:47,085 --> 00:01:50,545
A new hire gets a case the expert never saw.

27
00:01:50,545 --> 00:01:55,084
She predicts approve; the tutor reveals the expert's decision,

28
00:01:55,084 --> 00:01:56,826
in the expert's words.

29
00:01:56,826 --> 00:01:58,811
She picks approve anyway.

30
00:01:58,811 --> 00:02:04,372
The tutor intervenes before Save, and the deterministic interlock blocks the commit.

31
00:02:04,372 --> 00:02:06,172
Her mastery ladder moves.

32
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:11,115
The same rulebook guards AI agents.

33
00:02:11,115 --> 00:02:16,548
In our live run, a real Claude agent tried to approve this case through MCP,

34
00:02:16,548 --> 00:02:19,033
and was blocked with the expert's sentence.

35
00:02:20,888 --> 00:02:23,558
On Apprentice-Bench, with a simulated expert,

36
00:02:23,558 --> 00:02:27,471
8 targeted questions cut unsafe approvals from 14.

37
00:02:27,471 --> 00:02:29,050
6 percent to 1.

38
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:30,350
2 percent.

39
00:02:30,350 --> 00:02:36,770
Asking why at every step reaches zero, but costs 16 to 24 interruptions.

40
00:02:36,770 --> 00:02:41,949
Live, our first runs interrupted the expert 3 times; after the fixes,

41
00:02:41,949 --> 00:02:43,679
the re-run had 0.

42
00:02:43,679 --> 00:02:47,138
Still missed: live vision is below our bar,

43
00:02:47,138 --> 00:02:53,443
so the tutor uses the disclosed DOM channel, and production showed one 13.

44
00:02:53,443 --> 00:02:55,672
9 seconds host stall.

45
00:02:57,121 --> 00:02:58,143
LLMs infer.

46
00:02:58,143 --> 00:02:59,385
Experts confirm.

47
00:02:59,385 --> 00:03:00,650
Code enforces.

